{# The paragraph of one misplaced-record repair, rendered from its evidence; see quality/demand.py::build_demand. #}
{% macro misplaced(e, min_ratio, low_mw, high_mw) %}
The timestamp {{ e.duplicated_utc|utc }} carries two values while {{ e.moved_to_utc|utc }} has none. The value {{ e.moved_mw|num(2) }} MW fits naturally between the neighbours of {{ e.moved_to_utc|utc }}, and {{ e.kept_mw|num(2) }} MW between those of {{ e.duplicated_utc|utc }}. The total path is {{ e.cost_mw|num }} MW in this assignment against {{ e.swapped_cost_mw|num }} MW the other way round: a ratio of {{ e.cost_ratio|num(1) }}. {% if e.runner_up is none %}{{ e.moved_to_utc|utc }} is the only hour without a record whose two neighbours are known: no other destination is possible.{% else %}Of the {{ e.destinations }} hours without a record whose two neighbours are known, {{ e.moved_to_utc|utc }} is the best; the next best is {{ e.runner_up.slot|utc }}, where the total path would be {{ e.runner_up.cost_mw|num }} MW: a ratio of {{ e.destination_ratio|num(1) }} to the chosen destination.{% endif %} The rule moves a value only if both values are inside the physical bounds ({{ low_mw|num }} to {{ high_mw|num }} MW), if the ratio between the two assignments is at least {{ min_ratio|num(1) }}, and if the destination is unambiguous: the next best hour without a record whose neighbours are known costs at least {{ min_ratio|num(1) }} times the chosen destination's cost, or there is none. Otherwise nothing moves, the hour stays an unresolved duplicate, and the gate fails; the duplicate's evidence gives the reason and, when the hour holds exactly two values, both inside the bounds, and a destination was found, names the best destination and the next best if there is one (both, when the destination is ambiguous). The value is therefore moved, and the hour is labelled "repaired" in the canonical series.
{%- endmacro %}
